jobcenter-leverkusen-faq-earnings-twenty.tr:
What counts as income?

Basic support is paid only to people who cannot, or cannot sufficiently, cover their own living costs and those of relatives in their benefit unit. You must therefore use your own income and assets before receiving financial help.

Income generally includes all receipts in money or monetary value, for example:
• income from employment and self-employment;
• wage-replacement benefits such as unemployment insurance benefit, parental benefit or sickness benefit;
• income from letting, leasing, agriculture and forestry;
• maintenance payments and child benefit;
• capital income and interest;
• income from shares;
• pensions of all kinds;
• one-off receipts, such as tax refunds, severance payments and inheritances.

Amounts and allowances deducted from income are:
• the taxes on it;
• compulsory social-insurance contributions;
• legally required and reasonable private insurance;
• pension contributions subsidised under income-tax law;
• employment expenses, such as travel and maintaining two households;
• expenses for legal maintenance obligations;
• earned-income allowances.

Your gross income determines the size of your allowance.

The first €100 of earned income is not counted (basic allowance);

in addition, 20% of the portion of gross income above €100 and up to and including €1,000 is not counted;

in addition to those two allowances, 10% of your gross pay above €1,000 up to the earnings ceiling is not counted. For beneficiaries without a child the ceiling is €1,200 gross. For beneficiaries living in a benefit unit with at least one minor child it is €1,500.

Example:

You have a marginal job (up to €450 – generally no taxes or social-insurance contributions are paid). The following can be deducted:
• the flat amount of €100;
• plus 20% of the remaining €350, €70.
This gives an allowance of €170.

Please remember that you must always report income, even if you believe it may not be counted.

Example: your minor child delivers newspapers and receives €60 per month. This money is generally exempt and would initially have no effect on the benefit calculation. Nevertheless, remember to tell us that your child is working. A copy of the employment contract and the payslip is initially sufficient.

You can also obtain an initial overview using the allowance calculator.

