When does current housing benefit increase, decrease or cease altogether?
Normally housing benefit remains unchanged during the current award period. However, an increase is exceptionally possible on application if:
- the number of household members to be considered has increased, for example on the birth of a child;
- the rent or burden to be considered, less heating-cost amounts, has increased by more than 15%;
- family income has decreased by more than 10%;
and these changes lead to an increase in housing benefit.

Housing benefit is recalculated and may therefore decrease or cease if:
- the number of household members to be considered decreases to at least one household member;
- total income increases by more than 10%;
- the rent or burden to be considered, less heating-cost amounts, decreases by more than 10%.
The award becomes ineffective from the first day of the month in which no household member to be considered uses the dwelling any longer.
You are therefore obliged to notify the housing-benefit authority immediately of all the changes listed above. Failure to fulfil this obligation may be punished with a fine.

